---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/advanced/software-engineering-and-version-control">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Writing code is a fraction of software engineering. The discipline exists because real software
    <strong>changes</strong> — written by many people, over years, on machines you will never see, under
    requirements that mutate. Every practice in this lesson is an answer to one question: how does a
    program survive its own authors? Version control, tests, reviews and small increments are the working
    answers; Brooks, Knuth and the agile movement are the theory.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five practices that make software survive its authors" widget="equationStep" config={sweCfg} />

  <h2>Git: history as a first-class object</h2>
  <p>
    Version control's job is to make change reversible and collaboration mergeable. Git (Linus Torvalds,
    2005) implements it with unusual elegance: every <strong>commit is a snapshot</strong> of the whole
    project (actually a content-addressed tree — blobs, trees and commit objects each named by the hash of
    their contents, which is the data-structures lesson's hashing applied to source code: identical content
    is stored once, and any tampering changes hashes visibly). A <strong>branch</strong> is just a movable
    pointer to one commit — which is why branching is instant and free, and why the professional rhythm is
    a branch per feature, merged back through review. <strong>Merging</strong> combines parallel
    histories, reusing unchanged work automatically; when two commits changed the same lines, the
    <strong>conflict</strong> is Git refusing to guess — resolving it is a human decision that history
    records. <strong>Rebase</strong> replays your commits onto a new base for linear history — powerful
    and history-rewriting, which is exactly why it is forbidden on shared branches. The cultural layer
    matters as much as the machinery: small, single-purpose commits with messages explaining <em>why</em>
    turn the log into the project's diary; <code>git blame</code> and <code>git bisect</code> (binary
    search over history to find the commit that introduced a bug — again the logarithm) make archaeology
    routine.
  </p>

  <h2>Testing: converting belief into evidence</h2>
  <p>
    Untested software is a rumour. The professional hierarchy is the <strong>test pyramid</strong>: many
    fast <strong>unit tests</strong> (one function in isolation — cheap, precise failures), fewer
    <strong>integration tests</strong> (modules together — database, API), a handful of
    <strong>end-to-end tests</strong> (whole system through the UI — slow, brittle, irreplaceable). The
    pyramid's shape is an economics argument: unit tests find faults in milliseconds with pinpoint cause;
    a failing giant test tells you only that <em>something, somewhere</em> broke. The transformative
    practice is <strong>test-driven development</strong>: write the failing test first, watch it fail,
    make it pass, refactor — converting "I think it works" into "I can prove it still works, forever,
    automatically." That "forever" is the point: a test suite is a specification that re-executes on every
    change, and it is precisely what makes the last practice in this lesson — fearless refactoring —
    possible. Coverage (the fraction of code the tests touch) is a useful smoke detector and a useless
    target: 100% coverage of assertions-free tests proves nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>The shipping discipline: CI/CD and review</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Continuous integration</strong> merges every change into a shared mainline quickly (hours, not
    weeks) and runs the full test suite on a build server — so integration pain is paid in small doses
    instead of one agonising "merge week." <strong>Continuous delivery/deployment</strong> extends the
    pipeline: every green build is packaged (and, in deployment, released — often gradually, behind
    feature flags, with instant rollback). The pipeline is the team's discipline made mechanical: nobody
    can "just push it untested," because the machine does not allow it. <strong>Code review</strong> is
    the second pillar: another pair of eyes reads every change before merge — catching defects (studies
    consistently find review outperforms testing for certain defect classes), but equally spreading
    knowledge (no more "only Priya understands the billing code") and enforcing coherent style. The
    professional etiquette is specific: review the code, not the author; small changes get real reviews;
    nitpicks go to automation (linters) so humans review design and correctness. The industry's measured
    outcomes (the DORA research programme: deployment frequency, lead time, change-failure rate, time to
    restore) show high-performing teams are precisely the ones that deploy <em>more</em> often with
    <em>lower</em> failure rates — small batches win, empirically.
  </p>

  <h2>The economics: Brooks, debt, and the reader</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Brooks's law</strong> ("adding manpower to a late software project makes it later," 1975) has
    an arithmetic spine: potential communication channels grow as n(n − 1)/2 — 4 developers maintain 6
    pairwise channels; 9 maintain 36. Newcomers consume the veterans' time while ramping, so capacity
    arrives slower than the calendar demands. The whole field of process design (small teams, clear
    interfaces, agile iterations) is an attempt to bend that curve. <strong>Technical debt</strong> is the
    other ledger: every shortcut — duplicated logic, missing tests, "temporary" hacks — charges interest
    as slower future changes; and, like finance's debt, it is a <em>tool</em>: borrowing deliberately to
    ship a demo and repaying promptly is professional, while debt that is never repaid compounds until
    the codebase cannot be changed safely at all. Finally, the reader: code is read far more often than
    it is written, and debugging is harder than writing (if you write code as clever as you are capable,
    by Kernighan's rule you are not smart enough to debug it) — so <strong>simple beats clever</strong>,
    and Knuth's "premature optimisation is the root of all evil" completes the canon: make it work, make
    it right, make it fast — in that order, with measurements between the steps.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "agile means no planning." Agile planning is continuous rather than
  upfront: short iterations with working software each cycle, re-prioritised against reality — a response
  to the empirical failure of waterfall's big-design-first model on changing requirements, not an
  argument against knowing where you are going.</p>

  <h2>Git deep cuts, and the anatomy of an incident</h2>
  <p>
    Four commands separate daily Git from expert Git. <strong>git bisect</strong> automates blame: given
    a known-good commit and a known-bad one, it binary-searches history — you test each midpoint it
    offers, and 1,000 commits of regression collapse to ~10 checks (the logarithm again, pointed at your
    own mistakes). <strong>git revert</strong> makes a new commit that undoes an old one — safe on
    shared branches, unlike rebase's history rewrite. <strong>git cherry-pick</strong> copies one commit
    onto another branch (hotfixing a release line). <strong>Hooks</strong> (pre-commit, pre-push) run
    your checks inside the tool itself, so lint and test gates fire before bad history is even created.
    The deeper model beneath all of them: Git stores a directed acyclic graph of snapshots — which is
    why every operation above is just graph manipulation, and why "rebasing shared history" breaks
    collaborators (their commits now point at nodes your graph no longer references).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Incidents, worked.</strong> The discipline's maturity shows in how it handles failure, so
    walk a generic one. 02:14, pages fire: checkout times out. On-call runs the loop —
    <strong>mitigate first</strong> (rollback the deploy from 90 minutes earlier; checkout recovers in
    minutes), <strong>then diagnose</strong> (the rollback points at the deploy as cause; the commit
    changed a cache key format, and a shared cache now misses on every request — the database, sized
    for a 90% hit rate, melts at 0%). Fix lands behind a flag at 03:40. The <strong>blameless
    post-mortem</strong> the next day asks the engineering question — how did this deploy lack a
    canary? why did the alert fire on customer pain rather than the hit-rate metric? — and produces
    tracked actions (canary deploys, a cache hit-rate alert, a load test at 0% hit rate), not a villain.
    The pattern generalises: small batches make rollbacks possible; observability makes diagnosis fast;
    and the post-mortem converts each incident into permanent defences. Reliability engineering, like
    security, is a process — and the process is the software engineering.
  </p>

  <h2>Requirements and estimation: the honest beginning</h2>
  <p>
    Most failed projects fail before the first commit, at requirements and estimation. Requirements come
    in layers worth separating: <strong>functional</strong> (what the system does — "users reset
    passwords by email link"), <strong>non-functional</strong> (how well — "login under 200 ms at 10,000
    concurrent users"; reliability, security, accessibility), and <strong>constraints</strong> (the
    platform, the deadline, the budget). Non-functional requirements are the professional's tell:
    amateurs write only features, then discover in production that recovery, capacity and compliance
    were requirements all along. Estimation is the second honest battle. The empirical pattern (the
    "cone of uncertainty") is brutal: estimates made before design are routinely 4× wrong in either
    direction; estimates tighten only as the design exists. The professional responses: estimate
    <em>relative</em> size against known work (story points) rather than pretending to absolute days;
    re-estimate after each increment; track the ratio of estimate to actual to calibrate yourself — and
    when a stakeholder demands certainty before design exists, the correct answer is a time-boxed
    spike or a range, never a confident single number. Hofstadter's law ("it always takes longer than
    you expect, even when you take into account Hofstadter's law") is not a joke; it is the cone,
    stated with mercy.
  </p>

  <h2>What a professional repository looks like</h2>
  <p>
    Concretise the practices as an artefact you can imitate. A healthy repository has:
    <strong>README</strong> (what it is, how to run it — the ten-minute test: can a new engineer run the
    tests locally in under ten minutes?); <strong>one-command setup</strong> (containerised or scripted —
    manual setup steps are documentation debt); <strong>CI configuration</strong> in the repo (the
    pipeline is versioned like the code it verifies); <strong>linters and formatters</strong> (style
    settled by machine, so reviews argue about design); <strong>a test directory mirroring the source
    tree</strong> (finding the test for a function should not be archaeology); <strong>meaningful
    branches</strong> (feature/short-description, fix/issue-number) and <strong>protected main</strong>
    (no direct pushes; everything arrives reviewed and green). The commit convention itself is a skill:
    subject line in the imperative under 50 characters ("Fix off-by-one in cart totals"), blank line,
    then the why — the what is visible in the diff; only the why needs prose. None of this is
    bureaucracy: each element removes a category of coordination cost the team would otherwise pay
    forever, and the aggregate is the difference between a codebase people contribute to and one they
    route around.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Git at scale</strong> exposes its limits and their fixes: a monorepo holding millions of
    commits (Google, Microsoft) needs virtual filesystems and partial clones; trunk-based development
    pushes CI to its logical extreme (everyone commits to main continuously behind flags); the
    merge-versus-rebase debate is really a question about whether history should record intention or
    outcome. <strong>Formal methods</strong> are the discipline's high church: specifications as maths,
    proofs of correctness (the seL4 microkernel; AWS's use of TLA+ for S3), and property-based testing
    (generate thousands of random inputs, check invariants) as the affordable middle. <strong>Supply-chain
    security</strong> is the decade's hard problem: modern software is assembled from thousands of
    dependencies, so one compromised library (the 2024 xz-utils backstring saga is the canonical recent
    case) poisons everything downstream — signing, SBOMs (software bills of materials) and reproducible
    builds are the countermeasures. Open questions: can AI-generated code be reviewed at the rate it is
    produced (review is already the bottleneck); do the DORA metrics generalise to research code; and
    what does "technical debt" mean when much of the codebase was written by models? The practices in
    this lesson — history, tests, review, small batches — are the field's current answers, and each is
    under active revision.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Brooks, <em>The Mythical Man-Month</em> (1975); Knuth (1974); Chacon &amp; Straub, <em>Pro Git</em>; Google Engineering Practices documentation; DORA State of DevOps reports.</p>

